= Towards automation <sec:automation>

This section lists what is missing in the current tools to run the recipe of @sec:m-algorithm without hand work, and the bugs met on the way. Each item states how it was established: #byread means confirmed by reading the source at the place given, #byrun means confirmed by running a reproducer. Statements about the future behaviour of a tool are marked #expd.

*Tools.* gammaloop is the code that implements the $Rop$-operation. Idenso is its library of tensor identities, which contains the Dirac algebra (built on the tensor library spenso), and Vakint is its library of single-scale vacuum integrals. FeynKit is the model and diagram-generation layer of gammaloop; HepKIT is its Python interface, distributed with the Symbolica community package. FeynKit reads models in the conventions of UFO (Universal FeynRules Output). Diagrams are written as DOT files (the graph format of Graphviz).

*Code locations.* Paths in this section are relative to the root of the gammaloop repository, at commit `055fd0c1d` of the branch `bmhv` (branched off `feynkit`); line numbers refer to that commit. One location is on the branch `p_integral` (commit `dfb838402`) and is marked as such. The Python reproducers ran with the installed HepKIT wheel, which reports Symbolica 3.0.2 and is built from the `feynkit` branch. The four reproducers written for this section are called `r1` to `r4` below; they, the stand-alone codes of this report and the diagram lists are listed in @sec:supporting.

== What was automated in this report <sec:auto-status-text>

HepKIT generated the diagrams. On a JSON model of the regularised action of @sec:action (@sec:auto-generation), `generate_diagrams` enumerated all one-loop 1PI graphs of the classes of @sec:classes: 11251 component diagrams in 18 classes, which group into the 2975 multiplet-level diagrams of @tab:classes (@sec:auto-generation). These diagram lists serve as a completeness check. Everything else was done outside gammaloop, with two stand-alone Python codes (@sec:supporting):
- The integrands were written by hand from the Feynman rules of @sec:feynman-rules.
- A BMHV toolkit (sympy and numpy, cross-checked against explicit gamma matrices for $d = 4, 6, dots, 12$) did the BMHV numerator algebra of @sec:bmhv, the expansion $Top(dg(G))$ with auxiliary mass $MUV$ (@eq:uv-expansion), the tensor reduction @eq:tensor-red-split, the vacuum poles @eq:tadpole and the extraction of $N_0$, $N_1$ (@eq:N0N1). Its module for group theory, or hand calculation, gave the group factors.
- A plane-wave engine (numpy) computed the action of #bop on the bases of @sec:bases, the kernel @eq:closed, the normalisation analysis and the solution of the Slavnov--Taylor conditions. It evaluates local functionals on random plane-wave configurations with explicit components; an independent second implementation and a version in $d = 6$ check it.

The gammaloop $Rop$-operation and the Idenso Dirac algebra were not used for any BMHV quantity: the first rejects $g5$ in UV subgraphs (#it[M7]), the second has no BMHV algebra (#it[M5]). They were used only on vector-like test cases (QED self-energies, #byrun) to fix the conventions of the integration measure and of $Kop$.

